(window.MEANINGS_BY=window.MEANINGS_BY||{}).en=["The Bestower of immense blessings. This attribute belongs exclusively to Allah, meaning He is merciful and grants blessings to everyone — both disbelievers and believers. The attribute Ar-Rahman cannot be used for anyone other than Allah the Exalted.","The Bestower of subtle blessings.\n\nThis attribute is more specific, meaning 'showing mercy only to believers on the Day of Judgment'. This name may also be applied to others besides Allah, including the Prophet, peace be upon him. Allah's name Ar-Rahim is often mentioned alongside the attributes At-Tawwab, Al-Ghafur, Ar-Ra'uf, Al-Wadud, Al-Aziz, because Ar-Rahim is a specific mercy, while Ar-Rahman is a universal mercy.","The Owner of all things. Al-Malik is the true Sovereign. There is no sovereign besides Him. Therefore servants can only be servants to Him. A servant can never have two masters. That is why people must be servants not to others like themselves, but to the One Creator, the true Sovereign.","Free from all flaws, far above any imperfect attributes. Absolute holiness and absolute purity belong only to Allah.","Free from all defects. It also means the One who grants peace, tranquility, and comfort. Through the attribute As-Salam, Allah grants His servants peace, safety, and tranquility.","Al-Mu'min — the Giver of faith and security.","The All-Encompassing. Allah witnesses all states of His servants. Nothing remains hidden from Him.","The Prevailer over all. Nothing can overcome Him. All might belongs to Him alone. The might of strength and power also belongs to Him alone — He cannot be overcome. The might of withholding also belongs to Him — He is in need of no one. The might of wrath and victory also belongs to Him — nothing moves without His permission.","The meaning of the attribute Al-Jabbar is the Exalted Being, before Whom everyone humbles themselves. He relieves the poor of need, eases every afflicted person's burden, grants steadfastness and patience to those struck by calamity, and if they fulfill their duties, grants them immense reward in exchange for the trial. Broken hearts are healed through Him. The words in supplication 'Allahumma ujburni' express the mending of the servant and the removal of all that harms him.","Al-Mutakabbir — the One whose greatness and majesty are limitless. Before Him, all others are servants. He is above every evil and flaw, and above oppressing His servants. Whoever competes with Him in greatness, He crushes and punishes.","The One who creates that which has no prototype or likeness, in proper measure. Allah possessed the attribute of Creator even before creating the creation. He did not acquire the name Creator after creating it. Al-Khaliq is the absolute Creator.","The One who brings into existence from nothing. There is no flaw in Al-Bari's act of creation.","Al-Musawwir — the One who shapes creations, giving each thing its distinct form.","The One who forgives abundantly, pardoning the faults and sins of His servants through His grace.","The One who holds all creation in His grip, governing it by His command and subduing it by His power. Allah, with His mighty sovereignty, subjects to severe punishment those who deny Him despite the signs revealed proving His oneness, and those who oppress His creation.","The One who grants countless blessings. Al-Wahhab means abundant in giving, favor, and bestowal. In Arabic, the 'fa'al' form is used for emphasis. Allah is Al-Wahhab. He grants His servants from His great grace. He bestows blessings upon them continuously. He showers them abundantly with gifts. The treasuries of all things, the dominion of the heavens and earth, and the reins of all affairs are in Allah's hand. Allah disposes of His dominion as He wills.","The One who provides abundant sustenance. The Creator of provisions and their means. Ar-Razzaq provides sustenance to His creatures without any burden, without difficulty, without seeking anyone's help.","The One who opens many things. The One who opens the treasury of His mercy for His servants.","The One who knows everything. Knower of all that has happened and will happen, the first and the last, the apparent and the hidden.","The One who seizes souls. The One who withholds sustenance from whomever He wills. The One who constricts hearts (qabd means to grip, to withhold).","The One who releases souls into spaciousness. The One who expands the provision of whomever He wills. The One who expands hearts.","The Abaser. For example, He lowers the rank of disbelievers and sinners, humiliating them.","The Exalter. For example, He raises the rank of believers and the pious, making them honored and revered.","The Bestower of honor. Whoever He wills, He guides to the right path and makes honorable.","The Humiliator. Whoever He wills, He humiliates for having followed the crooked path.","The All-Hearing. He hears all voices despite the diversity of languages and the variety of needs. In His hearing, the secret word and the open word are equal. Allah's hearing encompasses all voices. Voices do not become mixed for Him. Hearing some voices does not distract Him from hearing others. The diversity of requests does not confuse Him.","The All-Seeing. He sees everything visible, sees all things even if they are extremely small and tiny. He sees the black ant crawling on a rock in the dark night. He sees what is beneath seven layers of earth just as He sees what is above seven heavens. He sees the movement of eyelids and the treachery of eyes.","The words Al-Hakam, Al-Hikam, and Al-Hakim carry the same meaning. This great name indicates that perfect judgment and perfect wisdom are firmly established for Allah. Perfect wisdom is established for Allah in His creation, His commands, and His law — He places everything in its proper place. No one asks Allah 'why'. No one can utter a word against Allah's wisdom. Allah's perfection and judgment belong to Him alone. He judges among His servants with whatever He wills. There is no one to overturn or review His judgment.","The Absolutely Just. Allah judges among His servants with justice in this world and on the Day of Judgment. He does not oppress anyone even by the weight of a speck of dust. He does not burden anyone with another's sin. He does not punish more than the servant's sin. He judges by restoring rights to their owners.","The word Latif has two meanings. The first is: knowing the finest and most subtle aspects of all things. That is, knowing extremely subtle matters, including secrets, what is within hearts, and hidden things. The second meaning is: showing the utmost kindness. That is, delivering various benefits to His servants and friends through His kindness and generosity, by ways they do not know. For example, the story of Prophet Yusuf and the story of Prophet Musa, peace be upon them, are examples of this. In both stories, affairs developed in the utmost secrecy and subtlety, and ultimately Allah's mercy reached the believing servants.","The All-Aware of everything. His knowledge perceives hidden things, secrets, what is concealed within hearts, what is hidden within grains, and atoms. He knows the fine details and is aware of the most subtle matters. This name is related to knowing extremely subtle, delicate, tiny, and highly concealed things.","Means the One whose anger does not flare up and who does not hasten to punish. He is forbearing toward His servants. Allah gives time to obedient servants so they may increase in worship and reward. He also gives time to sinful servants so that they may perhaps return to obedience and good deeds. If He hastened punishment for His servants, no one would survive the retribution. But Allah is forbearing, forgiving, giving respite and unhurried. Thus, out of His forbearance, He delays punishing the disbelievers. Out of His grace, He also delays the reward of the believers. In conclusion, the One possessing forbearance delays torment and punishment and does not withhold His grace and bounty from servants because of their sins. Rather, He gives provision to the disobedient just as He gives to the obedient, though there is a difference between them according to wisdom.","Magnificent and great to a degree that the mind cannot conceive. Pride, arrogance — that is, considering oneself greater than everyone else — and greatness belong exclusively to Allah. If someone claims greatness, saying 'I am great', Allah will put him in hell. Servants cannot know the measure and essence of the Magnificence that is Allah's attribute. If a Muslim knows that everything, no matter how great, becomes small before Allah's magnificence, and believes in this, he will be humble before Him and perform all types of worship exclusively for Allah, believing that only He alone is worthy of this worship.","The One who forgives much. Al-Ghafir and Al-Ghaffar have the same meaning. However, Al-Ghafur is used for emphasis. The word 'ghafur' means to cover, to shield. Forgiveness from Allah means shielding the servant from punishment. Allah's forgiveness is what stands between you and punishment. Thus, the characteristic of the servant is to commit sins, and Allah's quality is to forgive them. For the sake of forgiving sins, He commanded you to seek forgiveness. His command to seek forgiveness is because He knows of the weakness and heedlessness in humans. Forgiveness is a remedy for the weakness in humanity. If Allah did not forgive, how would we eliminate our sins, with what face would we meet our Lord?!","The One who gives much reward even for little deeds. The attributes Ash-Shakir and Ash-Shakur are also among Allah's names, meaning the One who does not waste the efforts of working servants and gives them abundant reward. Rather, He multiplies it several times over. For Allah does not nullify the deeds of the righteous. In His Book and the Sunnah of His Messenger, Allah has informed that He multiplies a single good deed from ten to seven hundred times. This is Allah's gratitude toward His servants. He enables the believers for the sake of His pleasure, then as gratitude for that, grants them goodness that no eye has seen, no ear has heard, and no human heart has felt. Doing so was not His obligation. He made it binding upon Himself only out of His generosity and benevolence. It was not obligated upon Allah to reward the obedient and punish the sinners. Rather, rewarding is from His grace. Punishing is according to His justice and wisdom.","Exalted in His loftiness without limit. Allah is Al-Ali by the exaltedness of His Being. He is elevated above the Throne and is higher than all universes, separated from them. He is Al-Ali and Al-Aliy, whose dignity is great. The attributes of Allah are Exalted and Great. His attributes are so great that no one's attribute comes close to them or resembles them. Indeed, the servants cannot fully comprehend, know, and understand even a single one of Allah's attributes. He is Al-Ali with supreme subjugation, meaning His wrath is supreme. Allah has subjugated everything. All universes have submitted to Him, all reins of creation are in His hand — whether each moves, stands still, or keeps silence, it does so by Allah's permission. What Allah wills happens; what He does not will does not happen. Believing in Allah's exaltedness above His creation brings about in the servant the glorification of Allah, standing before Him in humility and brokenness, declaring Allah free of all deficiencies and faults, sincerity in worshipping Him, and distance from taking partners and equals besides Allah, and it is a cause of these.","Greater than all things. Allah is Al-Aliyul-Kabir. That is, the One whose Essence, dignity, and wrath are great, Who is greater than everything. The One for whom pride is an attribute and greatness is a description. Allah said in a hadith qudsi: 'Might is My garment and pride is My cloak (meaning these two qualities belong to Me). Whoever disputes with Me over these qualities of Mine, I will afflict them with punishment' (narrated by Imam Muslim). Pride, arrogance — considering oneself greater than all — and greatness belong only to Allah.","The Perfect Preserver of all things. In Surah Hud of the Quran, it is said: 'Indeed, my Lord is Guardian over all things.' These two great names — Al-Hafiz and Al-Hafiz — indicate that Allah is characterized by protection and preservation. Allah's preservation of His servants is of two kinds: general and specific. General preservation: Allah preserves His servants by providing them with food, drink, and air, guiding them toward their benefits, necessities, and needs, and warding off evil, harm, and various unpleasant things from them. In this preservation, both the good and the bad, animals, and other things share. Allah has assigned angels to preserve the children of Adam. Specific preservation: By this, Allah preserves His close friends and allies from doubts that confuse their faith, trials, and desires that lead to destruction. Guard Allah — by not transgressing the limits He has set, refraining from what He has forbidden, and fulfilling His commands. Then Allah will preserve you — in your life, your religion, your wealth, and your children.","The Creator of all material and spiritual sustenance. Al-Muqit means: the Sufficient, the Preserver, the Provider of sustenance, the Giver of portions, the All-Capable. This name encompasses all of these. Thus, He encompasses the states of servants and what they need. He is capable of all things. He enriches the heart of whomever He wills from His creation with knowledge and faith. Al-Muqit is the Creator of material and spiritual sustenance, the One who nourishes and nurtures His servants. He created the nourishment that gives strength to the soul and body, and He alone provides the sustenance of all living beings. He also created the means by which people find their provision and commanded everyone to resort to righteous and pure means to meet their needs. Furthermore, Al-Muqit is the One who illuminates hearts with the lights of divine manifestation and grants spiritual nourishment.","The Sufficient and the One who will reckon the deeds of servants on the Day of Judgment. Allah is absolutely Sufficient. Only Allah is worthy of this attribute. Whenever any person offers you something, you still remain in need of something else. But if Allah provides you with safety, wisdom and knowledge, health and well-being, the blessing of family, righteous children, more than enough for your needs, inner peace, contentment, a prosperous lifestyle, a pleasant material life — then what He provides is sufficient for all aspects of material and spiritual life. Thus, if servants supplicate to Allah, ask for His help, and rely on Him, He will certainly be sufficient. His sufficiency is not for some parts as with humans, but He is Sufficient with the existence, continuity, and perfection of that thing. Abu Hamid Al-Ghazali said: 'Al-Hasib is the Sufficient. For whomever Allah is sufficient, He suffices. Sometimes a person serves you, yet you remain in need of an even greater service. He helps you, but now you need healing. But if Allah takes your matter in hand — it is not so. Be with Allah — you will certainly see His confirmation'.","Possessing magnificence in His attributes. When believers use the name of Allah, out of respect they say 'Allah jalla wa jalaaluhu' or 'Allah azza wa jalla', meaning 'Allah is all-powerful and great', 'Allah is mighty and great'. Thus, Al-Jalil also signifies might, self-sufficiency, and purity. Al-Jalil also means 'the One who has the right to command and prohibit'. He is so majestic that every greatness is insignificant before Him, and every height is lowly before Him.","The One who gives abundantly without being asked, without any compensation. Free from contradictions. Possessor of generous deeds and qualities. This attribute of generosity and munificence indicates that it is a firmly established quality of Allah. The word 'karam' encompasses all goodness and praiseworthy deeds — it implies not mere giving, but the most perfect meaning of bestowal. That is why scholars have said much about the meaning of this name: abundant goodness and gifts, perpetual charity and kindness, great dignity and majesty, purity from defects and calamities, bestowing honor, giving blessings, showing mercy, giving without compensation, giving without cause, giving to both the needy and the non-needy, fulfilling promises, accepting all needs whether great or small, not leaving those who turn to Him in vain, not abandoning those who come to Him humbly. Another thing said about the meaning of this name is: 'the One who pardons sins and forgives evil deeds'.","The One who watches without leaving out a single particle. If a believing servant believes in this name, its influence will clearly manifest in their faith, character, and conduct. When you feel you are being watched, that is the motivation to correct and discipline yourself. When a person feels they are being watched by a fellow human, they become disciplined — so what will it be like when they feel they are being watched by the Lord of the worlds?! Ar-Raqib watches the states of servants — their turning their faces and backs, their envy, hatred, sincere intentions, love of others, self-elevation over others, and scheming against others. Thus, whenever a person speaks a word, thinks something inwardly, or performs an action, He surely watches and knows of it.","The Answerer of prayers. Al-Mujib responds to the request of the one who asks, accepts the supplication of the one who supplicates, and grants the necessities of the one in need. Allah knows of a person's need before they even ask. But He encourages them to ask, so that they feel their closeness to Allah. Al-Mujib removes evil from His close friends and allies, lifts calamity from those He loves. Through His responding, great afflictions and huge problems are resolved, and even an enemy waiting to cause harm is eliminated. All of creation needs to ask from Him; without Him, their life cannot continue. Al-Mujib answers the one who asks, accepts the one who supplicates, and helps the one who calls out in anguish.","The All-Encompassing — 'the One who has encompassed all things with His vast knowledge and embraced all with His boundless mercy'. That is, His qualities, attributes, and all related to them are so vast that no one can praise Him as He deserves. Allah is as He has praised Himself. He is the One whose magnificence, sovereignty, dominion, grace, and benevolence are vast, and whose mercy and generosity are great. If people are poor, Allah through His grace makes them self-sufficient and wealthy. Allah is Al-Wasi' and Al-Alim — abundant in goodness and great in bounty. It is by the virtue of His name Al-Wasi' that Allah has made religion easy for His servants and has not commanded them with what they cannot bear.","The One who does all things with wisdom. The name of Allah 'Al-Hakim' appears nearly a hundred times in the Noble Quran. When two names of Allah appear together in one place, this takes on a special meaning. For example, 'Azizun-Hakim' means: Allah is powerful, but wise. That is, being powerful, He is also Al-Hakim at the same time. Allah's power does not lead to oppression. Rather, with the name Hakim and the attribute of wisdom, Allah uses this power in its proper place. This great name indicates that perfect judgment and wisdom are firmly established for Allah. His wisdom is perfect in His creation, commands, and law — He places everything in its proper place. No one can contradict Allah's wisdom. Perfection in judgment belongs to Him alone. He judges among His servants with whatever He wills. There is no one to overturn or review His judgment.","The One who wishes good for all. Allah wishes good for His righteous servants, loves them, brings them close to Him, and is pleased with them. The attribute of Allah 'Al-Wadud' means 'the Loving, the One who brings close, the Hearing, and the Responding'. This name appears in the Noble Quran in two places. 'Indeed, my Lord is Merciful and Loving' (Hud, 90). 'And He is the Forgiving, the Most Loving' (Al-Buruj, 14). If a servant truly knows the meaning of this name, its influence will manifest in all aspects of their faith, thinking, conduct, and life. Upon feeling that Allah wishes good for His servants, they will live happily and develop love for all of Allah's creatures. They will wish for the unbelievers to find guidance and for sinners to repent. Thus, whatever has been made subservient to humankind is because Allah wishes good through His attribute Al-Wadud.","His glory and dignity are immeasurably high. The words 'Al-Majid' and 'Al-Majid' mean 'glory, loftiness, height, and the utmost of perfection'. When the name 'Al-Majid' is applied to Allah, the highest degree of perfection and nobility is understood. Humans love perfection. Therefore, the human soul is filled only with the knowledge of Allah. If one chooses something other than Allah, they remain in distress and feel no comfort. Human life passes through several states. For example, after accumulating wealth and achieving desired material things, their value falls in one's eyes and they become worthless. But if one seeks Allah — no matter how much effort is expended — they remain in a happy state. For reaching the end of Allah is impossible — He is infinite and absolutely perfect. Whoever takes on the character of Allah's Al-Majid quality must be generous in all circumstances — a believer cannot be miserly.","The One who sends prophets to peoples. The One who sends resolve to people. The One who resurrects the dead. For example, if a person moves even a little toward Allah, Allah blesses that movement, encourages them, opens their heart, and helps them in obedience. If a person chooses a false path, the path of desires, a material path, or a harmful path, how does Allah help them move away from that decision? Allah constricts their chest as though it were ascending to the sky. 'Whoever Allah wishes to guide, He opens their chest to Islam. Whoever He wishes to lead astray, He makes their chest tight and constricted, as though ascending to the sky' (Al-An'am, 125). If you make a right decision, Allah helps you carry it out by opening your heart. If you make a wrong decision, Allah constricts your heart. The purpose of this is to prevent making bad decisions. It has become clear that Al-Ba'ith is 'the one who guides and sends'. Allah also sends help and assistance when His servants become helpless.","Present at all things and all. The Witness of all. Ash-Shahid means 'Allah watches over His creatures wherever they may be'. Allah is closer to His creatures than the jugular vein. Allah is a Witness through perfect encompassing of His creatures. That perfection is manifested through knowledge, sight, wise management, and power. If you move, He sees. If you speak, He hears. If you conceal something in your heart, He knows. If you stir, He sees. Ash-Shahid also means 'Allah has taken Himself and justice as witnesses'. 'Allah witnessed, standing in justice, that there is no deity except Him' (Aal Imran, 18). Thus, if Allah has witnessed to the absence of any deity besides Himself, He will be far from those who rely on other creatures and will discipline them for the purpose of teaching them manners.","The Unchanging, the Steadfast. The One who manifests truth. Allah is true, His word is true, His promise is true, His warning (threat of hellfire) is true. In Allah's Essence, attributes, and actions there is no doubt — all is reality. This truth is fulfilled in the world around us. For example, the sun rising at dawn, night following day, the earth being nourished by rain, His grace and generosity to His creatures, special mercy to His close friends, being with the patient, the pious, and the righteous, helping the oppressed even after some time, healing the broken, and removing harm from the helpless — all of this is genuine truth and reality. The primary meaning of 'haqq' is existence. The primary meaning of its opposite 'batil' is non-existence. If a promise is not fulfilled, then that promise is false. The promise of Shaytan is pure falsehood. The promise of Allah is genuine truth.","The One to whom all affairs are entrusted. Al-Wakil means 'the trustee of the servants' provision and affairs'. Thus, every affair of the servants is entrusted to Allah, and all affairs return to Him. For example, throughout life, a person encounters many problems, dangers, and illnesses. Why so? The wisdom here is so that the servant appoints Allah as their trustee, strives toward Him, relies only on Him, knocks on His door, and becomes a servant in need of Him. Only by appointing Allah as their trustee does a person achieve true reliance. Whoever tastes the flavor of relying on Allah attains true safety, tranquility, and peace. Allah created humans weak. If He had created them strong, they would not rely on Allah, would not supplicate to Him, and would not obey Him. Thus, human weakness is exclusively to their own benefit: they entrust all their affairs to the Mightiest and rely upon Him.","Extremely Powerful and Strong. Al-Qawi is the possessor of perfect power. He is capable of all things, and no weakness ever prevails over Him. He possesses such strength that all power disappears before His magnificence. Al-Qawi is the Victor who cannot be defeated; refuge is sought from Him, toward Him is the striving. If you are with such a Powerful One, do you have the right to fear anyone? As long as you are with Allah, who could ever prevail over you? If Allah is against you, who could ever stand by your side? O Lord! What has the one who found You lost? What has the one who lost You found?! 'They did not give Allah His true magnitude. Indeed, Allah is Powerful and Exalted in Might' (Al-Hajj, 74).","The Firm and steadfast in His strength. Sometimes strength comes suddenly — such strength is transient. But the firm, resolute, and eternal strength is called Al-Matin. When we apply the word Al-Matin to Allah, we use it in the sense of perfect power and strength. Allah's strength exerts influence without being influenced. Perfect power means His might is at the highest level — nothing on earth and in the heavens can weaken it. His strength is enduring in any place and at any time. O human, if you are with Allah whose quality is Al-Matin, you will feel strength and no one can harm you. You will realize that you are under the protection, care, and shield of Allah.","The One who loves, grants victory, and conducts the affairs of His people. Al-Wali in Arabic is the opposite of an enemy. When this name is applied to Allah, it means 'the One who conducts the affairs of all the world and creation, helps His friends, inspires through benevolence and grace, and loves'. For Allah to be the Wali of His servants means to be closer to them than the jugular vein. General waliyah means guardianship, care, and management of affairs. Special waliyah means being a friend to the believers, being close to them — that is, protecting them, loving them, and granting them victory. Allah said about this: 'Unquestionably, the allies of Allah — there will be no fear concerning them, nor will they grieve' (Yunus, 63).","The One praised with all praises. The word 'Al-Hamid' appears in the Noble Quran in 17 places. The word 'Al-Hamd' appears in more than 40 places. All praises belong to Allah. He is praiseworthy in all His attributes, actions, and decrees. Hamd is the greatest and broadest of all praises. Hamd is of two types: 1. Praising Allah for the blessings He has given — that is, thanking Allah for His goodness toward His servants. 2. The hamd that Allah Himself attributed to Himself. Imam Al-Ghazali said: 'Al-Hamid means 'the praised one'. Allah attributed hamd to Himself in eternity before creating creation. After creating creation, His creation praised Him.' Praise be to Allah, for He granted provision, intellect, wealth, family, health, safety, and the blessing of Islam. Whoever knows their Lord through the quality of Al-Hamid will be satisfied with praising and glorifying their Lord, without needing the praise of people.","The One who takes account of all things. Al-Muhsi encompasses everything in detail, and nothing on earth or in the heavens is hidden from Him. Al-Muhsi takes account of your outward breaths and your inward feelings. Al-Muhsi preserves the count of your acts of worship and is aware of your every state. Allah tallies up your deeds and prepares them for the Day of Meeting. For example, it is impossible to count how many days a person has lived, how long they have walked, how many words they have spoken. But Allah knows the subtle aspect, details, context, motivating cause, and purpose of every deed of yours. Everything you will see on the Day of Resurrection is what Allah has counted. Al-Muhsi is the One who accounts for the subtle aspects of affairs, knows the secrets, sees the manifest, and is aware of the hidden. If a person believes that Allah is counting their every movement, breath, deed, intention, and purpose hidden in the heart — their relationship with Allah will change for the better.","The One who brought all things into existence from the very beginning. Al-Mubdi' means 'the One who brings things into existence from nothing, without reference to any prior model'. But when a person invents something, they are certainly copying from the original of that thing. For example, diving was invented by observing fish underwater. An engineer also has three or four types of models revolving in their mind before constructing a building. But when Allah created the universe, He did not refer to any model and created existence from non-existence. Some mystics said: 'Al-Mubdi' sent light into the heart of His servant, and because of that light, their heart became illuminated.' For before receiving light, that person was lazy, idle, and anonymous. When Allah shed light from nowhere into their heart — they transformed from an unknown person into a respected individual.","The One who brings back to existence what has ceased to exist. Al-Mu'id returns creation from life to death. Death is the end of every living being. Then after death comes the true life. Thus, death in the worldly life is the beginning of the true life in the hereafter. Al-Mu'id also means 'resurrecting creation anew for the purpose of reckoning'. For in the resurrection there will be questions and answers, reckoning, and the displaying of deeds. The Restorer will question every creature about their deeds, words, and the blessings granted to them. The human body dissolves into tiny particles and faces annihilation. These very particles gather together and the person is created anew. Even if a person is burned as in Indian custom and their ashes are scattered in the open space or sea, Allah will resurrect them again. 'Say: He who created them the first time will give them life. And He is, of all creation, Knowing' (Ya-Sin, 79). The adab of the believer regarding this quality of Al-Mu'id is to remember their return to Allah and to repeat the name Al-Mu'id frequently.","The Reviver. He is the One who revives the dead. This name is appropriate for Him even before the resurrection of creation. Just as Allah is called the Reviver after breathing souls into the dead and reviving them, He is also called the Reviver before creating them and before giving them life. Just as He is the Creator before creating creation. Allah will resurrect the dead on the Day of Resurrection — hence He is named the Reviver. Thus, the quality of reviving is firmly established for Allah even before the resurrection of creation. Allah creates creation twice. 'Our Lord, You caused us to die twice and gave us life twice' (Ghafir, 11). Thus, in the first, He revives by creating from non-existence. In the second, He revives after death. There is also a third revival — Allah revives the dead heart. When a believer reads the Quran, tears flow from their eyes, their body trembles, their heart becomes excited, they love their Lord and obedience to Him, they listen to truth and speak truth, they remember Allah, they strive for Allah's pleasure — and with these things their heart is alive.","The Giver of Death. The One who takes the life of all beings. 'And it is He who will cause me to die and then give me life' (Ash-Shu'ara, 81). No person can kill another or bring a dead person back to life. If someone says 'I kill by means of execution', we say to them: 'Death occurs without execution — death takes place without damage to the bodily structure. But the execution you carry out inflicts damage on the body'. We must firmly believe that nothing in existence, other than Allah, can dare to put an end to life. A person does not die except according to His decree. If Allah is the Giver of Death, then what does the verse mean: 'The angel of death, who has been entrusted with you, will take your souls. Then to your Lord you will be returned' (As-Sajda, 11)? Some scholars answered: 'In truth, Allah created death. The act of death is also from Allah. But in the world of causes, death has been entrusted to the angel of death. The one who ultimately carries out death is none other than Allah alone'. Al-Mumit also means the One who, out of mercy toward those He loves, destroys the tyrants of fierce wrath, and who 'kills' fertile lands by withholding the rains.","The Living. He is always alive; He does not die. That is, Allah's life is eternal — it does not cease through death. Furthermore, Allah's life is pre-eternal — it was not non-existent before. The life of the One Allah is a special life unique to Himself alone, not given from another source like the life of servants. This life is pre-eternal and eternal. It has neither beginning nor end. Allah's life does not resemble the life of His servants in any way. The attribute of Allah, Al-Hayy, means eternally remaining and ever-living. When we say 'Allah lives', we understand He is eternally living. When we say 'so-and-so lives', we understand they are temporarily alive. The attribute Al-Hayy of Allah is eternal life within His own Essence. There is nothing like Him. But the life of creation does not reside in its own essence — rather it is in need of Allah's support. If Allah were to cut off His support, the creation would become a lifeless corpse. A creature lives by nearly a thousand causes. If Allah stops using even one of them — for example, stops the heart — life ceases.","The Self-Subsisting and Sustainer of all. The quality of 'Al-Qayyum' in Allah means that Allah, Blessed and Exalted, stands over every thing, and everything that stands does so only by His cause. In this, Allah's standing is unlike anyone else's — it is permanent. Al-Qayyum is called the Manager. Allah manages both Himself and His creatures. All of us are under Allah's management. Allah gives us life and manages us. The liver is such a mysterious organ that five thousand functions are assigned to it. If Allah were to cut off His support for it, it would become a tissue, a fleshy mass, like the liver of an animal sold in markets. The eyes, ears, tongue, heart, and lungs all function under Allah's management. If Allah were to stop supporting them, they would become like the eyes and ears of animals — cast into rubbish.","The Finder. The One who finds all that He wishes. No one can block Him in this. Sometimes a person says out of weakness: 'I searched for such-and-such thing but could not find it.' But Allah with the attribute Al-Wajid finds all things — nothing escapes from Him. He finds all that He demands and desires. When a person is characterized by a quality, it is relative. But when Allah is characterized by a quality, it is absolute. Existence is the opposite of non-existence. Our Lord is 'wajibul wujud', that is, eternal in both directions. Everything besides Allah is 'mumkinul wujud', meaning it was non-existence before and ends with non-existence again. Allah is Al-Qadim and Al-Baqi — nothing existed before Him or after Him. Al-Wajid also means 'one who never becomes destitute, self-sufficient'.","The Possessor of glory and honor. We earlier learned about the attribute of Allah 'Al-Majid' as 'incomparably high in glory and dignity'. The word 'Al-Majid' is used for hyperbole — meaning immeasurably great and extremely honorable. The attribute 'Al-Majid' is not mentioned in the Noble Quran but comes in hadith. However, the attribute 'Al-Majid' is mentioned in the Quran. The word 'Al-Majid' means honor, loftiness, height, and perfection. Al-Majid also means 'vast in generosity and self-sufficient'. Thus, knowing that Allah is the One whose generosity is vast, who is self-sufficient, whose honor and dignity are high and who bestows things, you too must adorn yourself with this quality and, to receive your share in it, be one who forgives people, is gentle with them, pardons them, and spends your wealth on the poor.","The Unique, the One. He is unique in His Essence, attributes, and actions. The word 'tawhid' is taken from this word 'Al-Wahid'. Every believer knows that the best thing a servant can learn is tawhid. Tawhid is the ultimate aim of knowledge. The content and essence of the calls of all prophets is precisely tawhid. Tawhid is religion itself. If you see anxiety and distress in yourself, it means your understanding of tawhid has weakened. Because such despondency arises from thinking that your future and provision are in the hands of people like you. If you know that you are under the protection of the Unique Allah — the Most Merciful of the merciful — you will take a step toward tranquility and peace. Tawhid is saying about everything that comes to mind that Allah is the opposite of it. Tawhid means that Allah is one in His Essence, attributes, and actions. Some gnostics said: 'Al-Wahid suffices you in place of everyone, but everyone cannot suffice you in place of Al-Wahid'. The difference between 'Al-Wahid' and 'Al-Ahad': 'Al-Wahid' — no partner. 'Al-Ahad' — no similar. Tawhid is of two types: 1. Tawhid of Lordship — Allah is one in His dominion: He is the Creator, the Provider, the Giver, the Preventer, the Elevator, the Humbler. 2. Tawhid of Divinity — worshipping Him alone, not worshipping anyone else, and not associating any partner with Him.","The One, without any likeness. The difference between Al-Ahad and Al-Wahid is that Al-Wahid means having no partner, while Al-Ahad means having no likeness or equal. Allah said about Himself in Surah Al-Ikhlas: 'Say: He is Allah, the One. Allah, the Eternal Refuge (Samad — needed by all, needing none). He neither begets nor is born. And there is none comparable to Him' (Al-Ikhlas, 1-4). Thus, Al-Ahad means He is unique in His Essence, attributes, and actions, and nothing and no one resembles Him, equals Him, or accompanies Him. This name is the purest expression of tawhid — signifying Allah's oneness in Essence, attributes, and actions alike. Muslims frequently recite Surah Al-Ikhlas, which contains this name, in their daily prayers, renewing their faith. The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, described this surah as 'equal to a third of the Quran', because it encapsulates the most essential creed of Allah's oneness. Whoever sincerely comprehends the meaning of Al-Ahad finds in their heart a tranquility that depends on no one and nothing else, for they rely solely upon the One.","The attribute 'As-Samad' encompasses many meanings: obeyed, the Great Master, without whom no affair is completed; no one is needed by Him, but everyone needs Him; even when all creation ceases to exist, He remains eternal; it also means 'without a belly', for Allah is far removed from things characteristic of humans like eating and drinking; the One sought during hardship. As-Samad means that creatures turn to Him and seek Him in every matter, turn to Him to set right their religious and worldly affairs, come bowing before Him when calamities and worries come, cry out to Him when grief and sorrow reach them, and there is no one besides Allah to seek help from when hardships and difficulties reach them. The servant who truly understands the meaning of As-Samad gains strength, honor, and reliance on none but Allah within themselves. As-Samad is the Master, perfect in mastery, the Noble, perfect in nobility, the Great, perfect in greatness, the Forbearing, perfect in forbearance, the Wealthy, perfect in wealth, the Powerful, perfect in power, the Knowing, perfect in knowledge, the Wise, perfect in wisdom.","The Possessor of unlimited power. He is capable of all things. Every matter is easy for Him. Allah has always been capable of all things. If He had not been capable from eternity, when and how could He become capable of everything? Al-Qadeer and Al-Muqtadir are like the attribute Al-Qadir and differ from one another in the superiority of their specific meaning. Allah's power is perfect — with His power He created existence from non-existence, with His power He manages their affairs. With His power He gives life and death, and rewards those who do good and punishes those who do evil. If He wishes for something and says 'Be', it comes into existence. With His power He directs hearts wherever He wills. If you preserve Allah's commands and refrain from what He forbids, Allah too will preserve you. Thus, one who well understands Allah's quality of Al-Qadir and adorns themselves with it always feels that they are with Allah. They unwaveringly follow Allah's commands. If they ask for something, they ask only from Allah.","Extremely Powerful. The attribute Al-Muqtadir is even more emphatic than the attributes Al-Qadir and Al-Qadir. Al-Muqtadir means that Allah dominates over everything to which He has given a portion of power. Al-Muqtadir means He dominates not only over the weak but also over the extremely powerful. Al-Muqtadir measures things with His knowledge and carries them out with His power. His knowledge and power are absolute. The attribute Al-Muqtadir jointly encompasses the qualities of Al-Qadir and Al-Qadir. Allah's name Al-Qadir means He measures everything with His knowledge. Allah predetermines every matter before creating and making it. Allah's name Al-Qadir means creating in accordance with prior knowledge through absolute power. Thus, the name Al-Muqtadir means the absolute execution of a prior firm, absolute, perfect, mighty plan. So where does the strength of a true Muslim come from? If a Muslim is together with Allah, who possesses the quality Al-Muqtadir, He will help them and grant them victory over their enemy. The solution to the Muslims' problem lies in relying upon the One with the quality Al-Muqtadir, without placing any intermediary in between.","The Advancer. He advances whomever and whatever He wills ahead of whomever and whatever He wills. This name is not mentioned in the Quran. It comes in a hadith narrated by Imam Al-Bukhari, Muslim, and Ibn Khuzaymah. If we say Al-Muqaddim about Allah, it means He advances things forward according to His will and wisdom. For example, He causes some to die before others. Or according to His wisdom, when afflicting with calamity, He advances one prophet ahead of another, one companion ahead of another, one believer ahead of another. 'Every nation has a term. When their term comes, they can neither delay it by an hour nor advance it' (Al-A'raf, 34). Thus, if you comprehend the meaning of this name and firmly believe that your life is in Allah's hand and the duration of your life can neither be advanced nor delayed, your soul will rejoice, you will become courageous, and you will live with honor and dignity, without humbling yourself or bowing your head before anyone.","The Delayer. He delays whomever He wills and whatever He wills from whomever and whatever He wills. Al-Mu'akhkhir linguistically is the opposite of Al-Muqaddim, meaning to postpone. So that servants may repent, He delays punishing them according to His mercy and wisdom. Allah is so Forbearing that if a person sins, He gives them the opportunity to repent. When Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, wanted to punish a thief, the thief said, 'By Allah, this is the first time I have stolen.' Then Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said, 'You lied. Allah does not expose your first sin. Being Forbearing, He delays exposure — you have stolen before this too.' In reality, if a person commits a sin and fully indulges in it without repenting, and takes pride in the sin they committed, their punishment comes swiftly. But if they commit a sin, Allah gives a respite. If that sinner repents for their sin, that regret is itself repentance. Allah only subjects to punishment the person who is fully immersed in their sin. Thus, Allah delays sending calamity and affliction upon His sinful servants according to His perfect wisdom and perfect mercy. If servants repent, Allah is their Friend. If they do not repent, Allah is their Physician. 'If Allah were to seize people for their wrongdoing, He would not leave a single creature on earth. But He gives them respite for a specified term' (An-Nahl, 61).","He was before all things — that is, He existed when all creation did not exist. Allah, who possesses the quality of 'First', created creation. That is, Allah's existence has no beginning. Allah is First because nothing existed in reality before Him. He is exalted in that He preceded all things in His Essence and dignity. He is not in need of anything else in any matter. Rather, everything needs Him in every matter. So be with the One who is First in all things — not with a powerless creature, but with the Mightiest of the mighty. Al-Awwal means that everything else traces back to Him, or He is the initial starting point in achieving a result. That is, Allah is the first cause of everything. For example, a chicken came from an egg. And the egg came from a chicken. This chain has no end. Eventually, the question arises: who created the first chicken? Allah, who possesses the quality Al-Awwal, created the chicken. Thus, Allah is the primary cause and the first of all things.","Even when all existence ceases to exist, He Himself remains. Allah is Al-Akhir. He is always with the oppressed. Sometimes a husband arbitrarily divorces his wife unjustly. Allah, honoring this righteous woman, makes her forget her former husband and grants her a second righteous husband as retribution. Thus, Allah Himself renders the final verdict. Sometimes the eldest son seizes all the wealth left by the father without giving it to his younger brothers. Allah, with His quality Al-Akhir, grants success after success to the affairs of the younger brothers, ruining the elder brother's affairs. As a result, the elder brother is forced to work for his brothers. Thus, Allah Himself renders the final verdict. Allah said about this: 'Or have they resolved on some matter? Then indeed, We too are resolving' (Az-Zukhruf, 79). A person who has not known Allah does not fear Allah. Our Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, who truly knew Allah, upon seeing dates in his place and greatly desiring to eat them, still did not eat, saying: 'O Aisha! Were it not for these being charity dates, I would surely eat them.' The help you gain from this name Al-Akhir is bonding with Allah and feeling yourself strong.","His existence is manifest and evident. He is manifest and dominant over all things. If we say Allah is Az-Zahir, we understand He is dominant over all in His loftiness, victoriousness, and height of dignity. He is victorious in every sense of perfection. His knowledge encompasses all things, both manifest and hidden. He is so manifest that His attributes are known through rational proof. Yunus (peace be upon him), left alone in the darkness of the whale's belly, the darkness of the sea, and the darkness of night, was brought to safety by Allah through the attribute Az-Zahir. Allah is also the One who reveals all hidden things through the attribute Az-Zahir. When someone asked Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, 'When did Allah come into existence?', Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, replied: 'He never began, that you ask such a question. He is always manifest, that is, visible.' A person asked a spiritual guide who heals hearts: 'Don't you long for Allah?' He replied: 'By Allah, no.' The person said: 'How so?' The guide replied: 'When did Allah ever disappear from sight that I should long for Him, since He is always manifest?!'","He is invisible to the eyes. He knows all hidden and secret things better than anyone. What is barred from sight is called 'batin'. When Allah is called Al-Batin, it means He is barred from visual perception. 'Eyes cannot perceive Him, but He perceives the eyes' (Al-An'am, 103). Thus, Allah cannot be seen with the eyes in this world. Whoever claims to have seen Allah is lying. But He will be seen in the hereafter. 'On that Day, faces (of believers) will be radiant, looking at their Lord!' (Al-Qiyamah, 22-23). Because the human capacity and characteristic of vision are unsuited for seeing Allah in this world. But the nature of humans in Paradise differs from their nature in this world. In Paradise, there is the possibility of seeing Allah. Allah remains invisible to the eyes in order to test and try His servants. Therefore, Allah described believers as 'those who believe in the unseen without seeing it with their eyes'. The intellect is the best means for knowing the invisible Al-Batin and attaining true knowledge of Him. Although there is no possibility of seeing Allah with the eyes, everything in the universe points to His existence. The supreme means for understanding this, as we said above, is the intellect. Everything in the world testifies to the fact that Allah is One.","The Governor and Owner of all things. The words 'Al-Wali', 'Al-Mawla', and 'Al-Wali' come from the same root, meaning 'the One who manages the affairs of His creation'. Thus, Al-Wali means 'the absolute manager and disposer of affairs, and the Nurturer of the universe'. To accomplish this, He must also possess the qualities of All-Knowing, All-Capable, and All-Aware. Allah is the One who possesses all these qualities. Possessing the quality Al-Wali requires skill, for knowledge is one thing and skill is another. Thus, human skill is 'occurrence' (that is, newly arisen), while Allah's skill is 'primeval' (existing from eternity). A person sometimes wishes to accomplish something but lacks the ability. Allah, however, 'is the One who does whatever He wills' (Al-Buruj, 16). But a believer who relies upon Al-Wali — the One who supervises all affairs, is the Guarantor, is skillful, and accomplishes whatever He wills — leads a tranquil life in any difficulty. Imam Abu Hanifa, who fully understood Allah's quality Al-Wali, upon reading the hadith 'Whoever seeks knowledge, Allah guarantees his provision', conquered the highest peaks in the field of knowledge. To Hasan al-Basri, one of the great Tabi'in who fully understood Allah's quality Al-Wali, came an order from Yazid, the governor of Basra. Then Hasan said, 'Allah can protect you from Yazid, but Yazid cannot protect you from Allah', and did not carry out that task.","The One who is above all imperfections. Everything is under His magnificence and dominion. There is no deity besides Him. Meaning there is no one who can overcome Him or dispute with Him. This attribute is mentioned in one place in the Noble Quran: 'He is the Knower of the unseen and the witnessed, the Grand, the Exalted' (Ar-Ra'd, 9). But some people panic, believing there exists some extremely powerful force on earth. In reality, that power which gives the ability to see through the pupil of the eye is in Allah's hand. If a person fully understands Allah's quality Al-Muta'ali, they will live with honor, dignity, strength, and courage. Imam Tabarani, in a narration from Abu Darda, relates the following. Allah said: 'I am the King of kings and their Owner. The hearts of kings are in My hand. If servants obey Me, I turn the hearts of kings toward them, making them merciful and kind. If servants disobey Me, I turn the hearts of kings against them, making them wrathful and harsh, and as a result kings subject them to various torments. Do not busy yourselves asking kings, but busy yourselves with remembrance of the Lord of the worlds and humbling yourselves before Him — then I will be sufficient for them Myself'.","The One who performs great goodness, carrying out benevolence in the superlative degree. If we apply this name to Allah, we understand absolute goodness. Allah's absolute bestowal is His goodness in this world and the hereafter, filling the heart with happiness, granting a person health and safety, and making their provision abundant. Al-Barr is Allah's goodness; al-Birr is what Allah bestows. The word 'al-Birr' also has meanings of 'goodness in this world and the hereafter' and 'piety'. Goodness in this world is guidance, health, peace of mind, tranquility, abundant provision, a righteous wife, righteous children, and lawful occupations. Goodness in the hereafter is being honored with Paradise, dwelling there eternally, houris, servants, gardens with rivers flowing beneath, varied fruits, gazing upon Allah's Face, and greatest of all, being honored with Allah's pleasure. Thus, with His quality Al-Barr, Allah bestows goodness upon His servants in this world and the hereafter. The servant's share in this name Al-Barr is to be occupied with all good deeds. Allah gathered good deeds in one verse: 'Righteousness is not that you turn your faces toward the east or the west, but righteous is one who believes in Allah, the Last Day, the angels, the Scripture, and the prophets, and gives wealth, in spite of love for it, to relatives, orphans, the needy, the traveler, those who ask, and for freeing slaves, and establishes prayer and gives zakah, and those who fulfill their promise when they promise, and those who are patient in poverty and hardship and during battle. Those are the ones who have been true, and it is those who are the righteous' (Al-Baqarah, 177).","The One who guides servants to repentance and abundantly accepts their repentance. At-Tawwab is a word used in Arabic grammar for hyperbole. If we apply this word to Allah, we understand quantity and type. That is, it is understood that Allah forgives no matter how great the quantity of sin or what type of sin it is, abundantly. The lexical meaning of the word 'tawba' is 'returning from one thing to another'. Repentance is the way to escape when a person is surrounded by mistakes. Repentance is a protective weapon when a person's misdeeds attack. Repentance is a guide that sets one on the right path when carnal desire attacks. 'The door of repentance was opened for them so that they might repent. Indeed, Allah is Accepting of repentance and Merciful' (At-Tawbah, 118). What does the phrase 'the door of repentance was opened' mean? It means He subjects servants to hardship in order to turn them toward repentance. If Allah had maintained their health continuously and continuously increased their wealth, they would have drowned in their desires. But in order for people to repent, He casts them into difficulty and hardship. Allah's mercy has no limit. If He were to leave servants persisting in sin, disobedience, and forbidden pleasures, they would become people of hellfire. But Allah, showing mercy and compassion to that servant, subjects them to affliction so they repent. Then, with His quality At-Tawwab, He abundantly forgives that servant's sins.","The One who avenges tyrants and the disobedient. If the word Al-Muntaqim were applied to a human, we would not approve of it. But applied to Allah, it takes on an entirely beautiful meaning. Therefore, it is counted among 'the Beautiful Names of Allah'. Allah, avenging the tyrant, stops him from harming others and puts an end to his oppression. The word Al-Muntaqim means punishment. Allah punishes for the purpose of discipline, disciplines for the purpose of happiness. If you encounter a criminal in worldly life who has killed, stolen, and done whatever he wished, harming people, and he is caught and imprisoned, people feel peace and relief from his imprisonment. Vengeance and punishment in this sense befits Allah's perfection. Sometimes you will say: 'It turns out that if you do something to this person, he changes for the better.' Indeed, Allah, by subjecting him to some punishment, transforms that bad person into a good one to an unbelievable degree. 'We had already sent messengers before you to their peoples, and they came to them with clear proofs; then We took retribution from those who committed crimes. And incumbent upon Us was support of the believers' (Ar-Rum, 47). 'So We took retribution from them. Then look how was the end of the deniers' (Az-Zukhruf, 25).","Means the Forgiver. Before speaking about this great name, let us speak about repentance and forgiveness. Allah opened the door of repentance for His servants, for Allah knows their errors and righteousness, good and evil deeds. When creating them, Allah also created them with desire. If this desire is not directed toward Allah and not joined with light from Allah, it will ruin its possessor. If Allah had not opened the door of repentance, human wickedness would have increased further, and one would have lost hope in Allah's mercy. Therefore Allah said: 'Say: O My servants who have transgressed against themselves, do not despair of the mercy of Allah. Indeed, Allah forgives all sins. Indeed, it is He who is the Forgiving, the Merciful' (Az-Zumar, 53). The word 'afw' lexically also means removing, erasing. Thus, the word 'afw' erases evil deeds and forgives sins. It is close to the name Al-Ghafur, but stronger than it. For maghfirah (forgiveness) forgives while leaving news of the evil. Afw, however, informs of its complete erasure. With afw, even the memory and pages of the angels who recorded it forget it. You committed a sin, but our Lord forgave it and did not punish you. Even so, the sin continues to bother and disturb you, and when you remember it, you feel shame before Allah. But if He forgives (afw), your sin is entirely erased from your record, the wind of forgiveness destroys it, and it is erased even from the angels' ledger. The word 'afw' occurs in more than thirty-nine places in the Noble Quran. On the greatest Night of Qadr, which comes once a year and is better than a thousand months, the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, commanded saying: 'Allahumma innaka 'afuwwun tuhibbul-'afwa fa'fu 'anni' ('O Allah, indeed You are Forgiving, You love to forgive, so forgive us').","Extremely compassionate and kind, the highest degree of mercy. Some might ask: 'What is the difference between Allah's name Ar-Rahim — the Merciful, and Ar-Ra'uf — the Kind?' The answer is that if calamity befalls someone, Allah shows mercy to them. But His kindness lies in keeping them away from all evils before calamity befalls them. Sometimes the name Ar-Ra'uf comes for protection and prevention of evil, while the name Ar-Rahim comes for remedy. Allah's showing intense mercy is due to His kindness. Because of His kindness, He calls a servant to repentance before they fall into sin. If they do fall into sin, then they become deserving of punishment. 'O our Lord, indeed You are compassionate and kind' (Al-Hashr, 10). The proper conduct regarding this name is that we should mention it often out of love for Allah. If we wish to be characterized by this name, we should prevent people from falling into sin. Here we should engage not in remedy but in prevention of the illness. It is especially important to apply this with our children.","The Owner of Sovereignty. He carries out affairs in this world as He wishes. There is no one who can overturn His decree or change His judgment. In the Noble Quran, this name appears as Al-Malik, Al-Malik, Al-Malik, and Malikul-Mulk. 'Say, O Allah, Owner of Sovereignty, You give sovereignty to whom You will and You take sovereignty away from whom You will. You honor whom You will and You humble whom You will. In Your hand is all good. Indeed, You are over all things Competent' (Aal Imran, 26). Imam Al-Ghazali said Malikul-Mulk means: 'The One who carries out His will in His domain, that is, in His kingdom, however He wishes.' One of the commentators said Malikul-Mulk means 'the true owner of sovereignty'. Therefore in a hadith qudsi Allah said: 'I am the Owner and Master of kings. The hearts of kings are within My command. If servants obey Me, I turn the hearts of kings toward them with mercy and kindness. If servants disobey Me, I turn the hearts of kings against them with wrath and fury. Do not busy yourselves cursing your kings, but pray for their correction, for their well-being is tied to your well-being.'","The Possessor of Majesty and Generosity. The Possessor of Honor and Bestowal. We can divide the names of Allah into two parts. One part points to His power, the other to His perfection. The name 'Al-Ikram' encompasses all types of perfection. The name 'Dhul-Jalal' encompasses all manifestations of power. This name is mentioned in two places in the Noble Quran — verses 27 and 78 of Surah Ar-Rahman. Imam Ar-Razi said: 'Al-Ikram' is close to the meaning of bestowal, but Al-Ikram has a more specific meaning. For Allah may also bestow blessings upon someone He does not honor. For example, Allah does not love the disbeliever. Yet He bestows upon them wealth, health, children, homes, and vehicles. Thus, all of this is a gift, not honor. But what He grants to believers is honor. Allah's honoring is of two types. The first is honor that quickly arrives in this world. The second is honor deferred to the hereafter. According to narrations, the name 'Dhul-Jalali wal-Ikram' is among 'the Greatest Name'. It is narrated from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him: a man performed prayer and then supplicated: 'Allahumma inni as'aluka bi-anna laka-l-hamdu la ilaha illa anta-l-mannanu badi'u-s-samawati wa-l-ardi ya dhal-jalali wa-l-ikram ya hayyu ya qayyum'. Then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: 'Indeed, he called upon Allah with the Greatest Name. If supplicated with it, the supplication is accepted. And if asked with it, it is given'.","The One who grants help to the oppressed and punishment to tyrants through justice. If this attribute is applied to Allah, it means justice in His judgment. There are many types of oppression. Oppression is darkness on the Day of Judgment. If your strength pushes you to oppress people, remember Allah's power over you. The stronger a person's knowledge becomes, the stronger their fear of Allah grows. But if a servant, full of power, says 'I do whatever I want' and turns to using unjust force, they will be subjected to Allah's wrath. Al-Muqsit means 'the One who restores the right of the oppressed from the oppressor'. Allah said: 'And if you judge, judge between them with justice. Indeed, Allah loves those who act justly' (Al-Ma'idah, 42). Sometimes you might blindly love one person while blindly disliking another. But fairness demands that you love with justice and dislike with justice. A friend of mine said: 'I have two sons, one is disobedient and the other is righteous. I give the righteous son a greater share than the disobedient one.' I said without hesitation: 'Don't do that! If you are unjust to the second, you will become the cause of him persisting in sin and becoming rebellious. Be just between the two, then what you give will also be blessed.' Regarding this blessing, one hadith says: 'Whoever does not pray on time, Allah removes blessing from their life. The best of deeds before Allah is prayer performed on time.'","The Gatherer. The One who gathers all truths. The One who gathers people on the Day of Judgment. The reason it is called the Gathering Day is that on that day Allah gathers the tyrant and the oppressed, the strong and the weak, the giver and the receiver, the arrogant and the humble, in order to restore each one's right from one another. On that day, in order to reward those who performed obedience and worship and punish sinners, He gathers all creation from beginning to end, jinn and humans, the inhabitants of the heavens and earth. Al-Jami' means that Allah gathers all perfection within Himself — in His Essence, attributes, and actions. Al-Jami' is also understood to mean that He will gather creation that has broken into fragments on the plain of resurrection on the Day of Rising. Sometimes, if a person loses something, they supplicate: 'O Allah, indeed the Gatherer of people on a certain day! Gather me with my need.' And, if Allah wills, their need is fulfilled. The name Al-Jami' appears in several verses of the Noble Quran. In verse 9 of Surah Aal Imran, it says: 'Our Lord, surely You will gather the people for a Day about which there is no doubt. Indeed, Allah does not fail in His promise.' Regarding the servant's conduct with this name, Imam Al-Ghazali said: 'This is gathering outward discipline in the limbs together with inward truths in the hearts. Whoever's spiritual insight is perfect, their conduct too becomes beautiful. This state is called gathering.'","Free of need. He does not need anyone or anything. The characteristic of the Creator is that He needs nothing in every respect. The characteristic of creation is that it needs everything. Allah said in verse 38 of Surah Muhammad: 'And Allah is Free of need, while you are the needy.' Often in the Quran, the word Al-Ghani (free of need) comes close to the word Al-Hamid (praised). For Allah does not need His servants, while creatures praise Him. But a person can be wealthy and free of need without dependence on anyone, yet be rude, arrogant, haughty, and disliked. But the Creator of heaven and earth is perfect and free of need. Allah Himself needs nothing, and at the same time He provides His creation with food, drink, and air. Human self-sufficiency is relative. For a person, while being self-sufficient, simultaneously needs countless things. Allah, however, needs nothing from His creation in His Essence, attributes, or sovereignty. All creation needs His blessings and favor. He is absolutely and truly free of need. To be characterized by this quality of Allah means always to express one's poverty and need before Him, and when accomplishing something, to say not 'I accomplished this by my own mind or knowledge' but 'I achieved this because of Allah's grace, help, and enabling'.","The Enricher, the One who makes free of need. He makes free of need whoever He wills among His servants. You see some people who, despite being extremely sharp and intelligent, possess no wealth at all. You see other people who, despite being simple and ordinary, possess complete abundance. This is precisely Allah's name Al-Mughni, understood to mean He makes whoever He wills free of need. Al-Mughni makes His servants free of need through knowledge of Himself. For example, Rabi'ah served the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, for several days. He said, 'Ask, if you have a need.' Rabi'ah said, 'Give me some time.' After a few days he asked, 'O Rabi'ah, what happened?' He replied, 'I ask to be your companion in Paradise.' He asked, 'Who taught you this?' He replied, 'By Allah, no one taught me this. But knowing that the world is transient and has no real worth, I simply asked for the eternal abode in Paradise and to be with you in Paradise.' Allah made this known to that person and made him free of need. Allah sometimes makes free of need through wealth, or through knowledge, or through health, or through a righteous wife, or through a dwelling. Jibril, peace be upon him, came to Ibrahim, peace be upon him (while he was being cast into the fire), and asked, 'Do you have any need?' Ibrahim, peace be upon him, asked, 'From you?' Jibril, peace be upon him, said, 'No, from Allah.' Ibrahim, peace be upon him, said, 'His awareness of my situation makes me free of need to ask.'","The Preventer. Also means the Protector. He protects you from enemies, foes, the descent of evil, harmful things, as well as obstacles in religion and worldly life. Feeling protected is a wonderful sensation, meaning a person is under Allah's protection and shelter. Sometimes a person gets into the most modern vehicle and sets out on a journey. But cannot reach their destination. Sometimes, riding an old vehicle, they safely reach their destination. They feel safety and peace within themselves because they are under protection. Allah instills fear in the hearts of polytheists because they committed shirk. A believing servant, however, always lives in an elevated spirit and lofty feeling. For they are under the protection of the One characterized by Al-Mani'. He protects them from enemies and foes. The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, went to the Banu Nadir tribe to remind them of the agreement between them, and sat in one place. Those present conspired and agreed to drop a boulder from above to kill the Messenger of Allah. At that moment, revelation came down informing him of this. The Messenger of Allah stood up and left his place, remaining safe from death. One who is characterized by this name must not ask anyone besides Allah, for both the Giver and the Preventer are He alone.","The One who harms whomever He wills. Ad-Darr is the opposite of the word 'benefit'. When does a person become a hypocrite, engage in ostentation, become afraid, bow the head, or be humiliated? A person encounters these things when they hold the notion that another person like themselves can benefit or harm them. But if they believe that only Allah gives both benefit and harm, they walk with their head held high, in honor. The reason for all strength, honor, and courage is believing that there is no one besides Allah who benefits or harms. A believing servant submits to Allah's decree and is patient on His path. If a believer shows patience with Allah's decree, Allah places tranquility in their heart. How should one understand Allah's causing harm? For example, no matter how much a surgeon loves their own child, if the child has appendicitis, they administer anesthesia, cut open the abdomen with a surgical knife, and, causing pain, remove the appendix. But the father does this solely for the sake of his child's health. Allah's causing harm should be understood in exactly this same sense. Allah said about this: 'And to Allah belong the best names, so invoke Him by them and leave those who deviate concerning His names' (Al-A'raf, 180). The servant's share in this name is that a believer should stay away from misguided people, in order to benefit Allah's servants without causing harm. As well as not hoping in anyone besides Allah, not fearing anyone, and being completely reliant upon and submitted to Allah.","The One who gives benefit. An-Nafi' is the opposite of the word 'harm'. When does a person become a hypocrite, engage in ostentation, become afraid, bow the head, or be humiliated? A person encounters these things when they hold the notion that another person like themselves can benefit or harm them. But if they believe that only Allah gives both benefit and harm, they walk with their head held high, in honor. In many religious sources, the names 'Ad-Darr' and 'An-Nafi'' must be used together for a simple reason. That simple reason is that Allah causes harm in order to bring benefit. That is, if He subjects some people to severe illness or economic difficulty, it is so that servant may return to Allah and repent. Some mystics used to supplicate: 'O Allah! I ask, taking Your name An-Nafi' as witness. I will not rely on anyone besides You, O One with the names Al-Wali and Al-Wasi'.' Some scholars said: 'Ad-Darr' and 'An-Nafi'' — these two names and attributes indicate perfect divine power. There is no one who causes harm, gives benefit, brings evil, or does good, except by Allah's will.' Allah said: 'Say, all is from Allah' (An-Nisa, 78). Imam Al-Qushayri said: 'The names Ad-Darr and An-Nafi' are among the beautiful names of Allah, and in the meaning of both there is an indication of tawhid.' In verse 188 of Surah Al-A'raf of the Noble Quran, it says: 'Say, I hold not for myself [the power of] benefit or harm, except what Allah has willed. If I knew the unseen, I would have secured much benefit, and no harm would have touched me. I am only a warner and a bringer of good tidings to a people who believe.'","Manifest by Himself and making others manifest. The lexical meaning of the word 'nur' is radiance and light. Allah, through the Noble Quran, teaches the truth of life, explaining what is lawful and forbidden, good and evil, truth and falsehood. In verse 15 of Surah Al-Ma'idah, it says: 'Indeed, there has come to you from Allah a light and a clear Book.' Thus, this Quran is precisely light. Light is of two types: 1. Rational. 2. Special. Radiance resembling the light of the sun and moon is called special light. The Quran, which contains divine teaching, is called rational light. You need the light of the sun for your eyes to see things. You need Allah's light for your mind to know the truth of things. No matter how sharp and talented a person is, if they do not receive help from Allah's light, they are truly blind. Allah named Himself Light, for light comes from Him. He illuminates horizons with stars, bodies with the marks of worship, and hearts with evidence and proofs. If a person becomes attached to Allah, light appears on their face. The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, used to recite a supplication in order to obtain that light: 'Allahumma-j'al fi qalbi nuran wa fi lisani nuran waj'al fi sam'i nuran waj'al fi basari nuran' ('O my Lord, place light in my heart, place light in my tongue, place light in my hearing, place light in my sight').","The Guide. Through His grace, He guides whomever He wills. Guidance means directing toward the right path. If you reflect on the creation of the earth and heaven, you take a broad step toward knowledge of Allah. The universe guides us toward Him. The first and greatest of guidances is seeing Allah's knowledge, wisdom, power, wealth, and forbearance in the universe. Allah equipped you with the sense of smell. Allah gave you the ability to think, knowledge and experience, and five sensory organs. Allah directs all animals through instinct toward their own benefit. There are four types of guidance. The first is general guidance, directing a person regarding food, clothing, and building a home. The second is the guidance of faith, guiding toward faith and truth through the Quran. The third is the guidance of enablement (tawfiq), about which Allah said: 'We narrate to you their story in truth. Indeed, they were youths who believed in their Lord, and We increased them in guidance' (Al-Kahf, 13). The fourth is guidance to Paradise, about which Allah said: 'Those who are killed in the cause of Allah - He will never let their deeds be wasted. He will guide them and amend their affairs. And admit them to Paradise, which He has described to them' (Muhammad, 46). 'Fear Allah, and Allah will teach you. Allah is Knowing of all things' (Al-Baqarah, 282).","The Originator of things without any precedent, having no resemblance. But if a person invents something, they undoubtedly imitate Allah's creations. One who accomplishes something no one has done before is called 'abda'a' in Arabic. The word 'bid'ah' (innovation) is taken from the same root. Bid'ah has two meanings. Lexically, every new thing is called bid'ah. In Sharia, however, innovation refers to creating something not present in religion. Committing religious innovation is a forbidden act. The name Al-Badi' calls for not creating innovation in creed, worship, and spiritual conduct. For a person who creates innovation in religion thereby abandons some sunnah. Scholars defined bid'ah as: 'That which has no basis in the Book of Allah, the Sunnah of His Messenger, and the consensus of the community.' We are permitted to create novelty and invention in Allah's creation. For example, innovation and invention are permitted in agriculture, plant cultivation, animal husbandry, making colors, alloying metals. But in religion we have absolutely no right to innovate. Another meaning of the word Al-Badi' is that He has no equal, no resemblance, nothing like Him in Essence, attributes, actions, or creation. Allah created everything from non-existence. The servant's share in this name is not to be an innovator.","The Everlasting. He exists permanently; transience does not touch Him. Thus, everlastingness is the opposite of transience. What is understood by Allah's everlasting things is obedience to Allah and waiting for His reward. Sometimes obedience and worship are burdensome and difficult. But their hardships disappear while their reward remains everlasting. Carnal pleasure, however, disappears while its punishment remains everlasting. Another meaning of Al-Baqi is everlasting righteous deeds. 'Wealth and children are the adornment of worldly life. But the everlasting righteous deeds are better with your Lord for reward and better for hope' (Al-Kahf, 46). Saying 'Subhanallahi wal-hamdulillahi wa la ilaha illallahu wallahu akbar' is also among the everlasting righteous deeds. In reality, Al-Baqi is understood to refer only to Allah Himself. When our Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, passed away, Abu Bakr As-Siddiq said: 'Whoever was worshipping Muhammad - that person has passed away. Whoever was worshipping Allah - Allah is alive, He does not die.' Allah is everlasting. The attribute of everlastingness is among the essential attributes. Imam Al-Ghazali said: 'Al-Baqi means He is wajib-ul-wujud (necessarily existent) in His Essence.' If wajib-ul-wujud is applied to the future, it becomes everlasting; if applied to the past, it becomes pre-eternal. Imam Ar-Razi said: 'Allah is wajib-ul-wujud, that is, unworthy of ceasing to exist.' Another meaning of the word Al-Baqi is: 'His existence has neither beginning nor end.'","The One who remains everlasting even when all existence ceases. In worldly life, everyone perishes — only the One characterized by Al-Warith remains alive. For Allah is living and everlasting, the Inheritor. Wealth is temporarily given into your hands as a trust. Its purpose is solely to test which path you will take. 'Why do you not spend in the way of Allah, while to Allah belongs the heritage of the heavens and the earth?' (Al-Hadid, 10). The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, did not leave behind dirhams and dinars as inheritance — he left behind the knowledge of this religion as inheritance. Whoever obtains this knowledge has obtained a full share. On the Day of Judgment, Allah will call out: 'To whom does all sovereignty belong?' All creation will answer: 'To Allah, characterized by Al-Wahid and Al-Qahhar.' Every 50 years, real estate, shops, and farmland change to new owners. This continues until Allah alone becomes the sole Inheritor of heaven and earth. Our connection to this name is that the things in our hands do not belong to us — they will pass to others, and ultimately Allah Himself will be their Inheritor. What is given to us is only as a trust; we are merely its administrators, and one day we will be strictly questioned about it.","The One who guides toward the right path. The attribute Ar-Rashid, when applied to Allah, means the One who guides absolutely toward the right path. Allah's knowledge, power, and mercy are absolute. If a person believes that Allah's attributes are absolute and everything is under His command, tranquility will reign in their soul. Allah bestows health, intelligence, wealth, and beauty upon many of His creatures. But He bestows tranquility only upon His sincere believing servants. Corruption arises through the misuse of blessings that Allah has bestowed. Allah, with His quality Ar-Rashid, guides you toward the firmest and most correct path. Rashid means walking on the path with soundness and correctness, without going astray, without leaning to the right or left, taking the shortest way toward a pure goal. A believing person's outlook is sound. For Allah has placed light in their heart. Through that light, they know truth as truth, they enter that true path, and they attain the happiness called 'rashid' - walking on the right path. 'Whoever Allah guides is the [rightly] guided, and whoever He sends astray - you will never find for him a protecting guide' (Al-Kahf, 17). 'He grants wisdom to whom He wills, and whoever has been granted wisdom has certainly been given much good. And none will remember except those of understanding' (Al-Baqarah, 269). Guidance toward the right path is of four types: 1. Guidance that benefits a person. 2. Guidance obtained through revelation. 3. Guidance obtained through Allah's enablement. 4. Guidance for the purpose of attaining Paradise.","Extremely patient. He does not hasten to punish the disobedient. Sometimes, seeing that disbelievers who deny Allah persist in sins and oppress others, those weak in faith say: 'Where is Allah? Why doesn't He punish them?' Allah said about this: 'Never think that Allah is unaware of what the wrongdoers do. He only delays them for a Day when eyes will stare [in horror]' (Ibrahim, 42). Allah is not unaware of this; He simply lets a person go, feigning ignorance, until they reach a certain level of danger. According to His perfect wisdom, He delays punishment and exercises patience. Some might ask: 'What is the difference between Allah's quality Al-Halim and As-Sabur?' The answer is that the name As-Sabur focuses only on delaying punishment. The name Al-Halim, however, delays punishment and in the end even forgives it. A person's share in Allah's name As-Sabur is to exercise patience for the sake of their Lord. For example, a weak person endures a strong person's oppression and says: 'Hasbiyallahu wa ni'mal wakil' - 'Allah is sufficient for me, and how excellent a Trustee is He'. But true patience, pleasing to Allah and elevating one toward Him, is the ability to punish yet choose forgiveness instead. For example, on the day of the conquest of Mecca, the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, asked the Quraysh: 'What do you think I will do to you?' They replied: 'You are a noble man, son of a noble man.' Then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: 'Go, you are free' - and did not punish them. Patience in obedience and worship leads to success. Patience in remaining in sin and disobedience leads to humiliation, and then to the grave. To have a share in Allah's name As-Sabur, you too must exercise patience with your wife, children, neighbors, relatives, work partners, and everyone around you."]
